  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1138 PM CDT TUE AUG 25 2026\n\nSurface observations and earlier visible satellite imagery \nindicated a few areas of surface boundaries and convergence across\nthe area. While the weather pattern and nighttime stability will \npromote fair-weather conditions overall, some high-res guidance \nshow the potential for a very sparse occurrence of short-lived \nshowers tonight, likely associated with these features; nothing \nsignificant, just a don't-be-surprised mention. \n\nFrom Wednesday through Friday, a weakness in the 500 mb ridge \nwill exist thanks to longwave troughing near the Great Lakes \nregion. This setup will allow for several mid-level impulses to \nmeander across Alabama and its vicinity. Associated lift and an \nincrease in dew points/moisture will promote periods of showers \nand thunderstorms, especially from afternoon to evening. Ensemble \ndata continue to suggest scattered coverage on Wednesday, followed\nby potentially more widespread activity on Thursday and Friday as\na surface front approaches from the north. Shower and thunderstorm\ncoverage during the weekend seems to decrease as 500 mb ridging \nbecomes a bit more defined. \n\nNo temperature extremes are noted through the forecast period, \nfeaturing seasonal highs and lows alongside mugginess.\n\n89^GSatterwhite",
